/-
  Replication through an object store (P3, D50).

  A remote holds immutable bundles under `bundles/<level>-<sha256>`. A bundle is one canonical
  S-expression: branch claims, then objects and metadata, each sorted by hash:

    (bundle (branch "main" "<causal>")… (base "main" "<causal>")… (obj "<hash>" <form>)… (meta "<hash>" <form>)…)

  A `base` names a head of the branch on the remote that the claim builds on (D167); readers that
  do not know it skip it.

  - `push` uploads one level-0 bundle with the objects the remote lacks and a claim for the
    branch head. Names are content hashes, so writes never conflict and nothing is overwritten.
  - `pull` imports bundles not seen before and sets the tracking branch `<remote>.<branch>` to the
    claimed heads that no other claim descends from. Two concurrent pushes leave two heads,
    `<remote>.<branch>` and `<remote>.<branch>.2`, to merge as local branches are.
  - `compact` collects history into strata (sedimentree): a causal node whose hash starts with `k`
    zeros closes a level-k stratum, which holds what that node reaches and no earlier boundary
    does. Boundaries come from hashes and bundles are canonical, so clients compacting the same
    history write byte-identical bundles. A smaller bundle is deleted once compacted bundles hold
    all its objects; claims that are still heads move to a claims-only bundle first.
  - Nothing else is ever deleted: the whole history stays on the remote, as git keeps it (D73).

  Locally, `remotes/<name>/url` names the remote, and `remotes/<name>/seen/<bundle>` lists a seen
  bundle's claims and hashes, so a pull reads each bundle once.
-/

/-
  Replication through an object store (P3, D50).

  A remote holds immutable bundles under `bundles/<level>-<sha256>`. A bundle is one canonical
  S-expression: branch claims, then objects and metadata, each sorted by hash:

    (bundle (branch "main" "<causal>")… (base "main" "<causal>")… (obj "<hash>" <form>)… (meta "<hash>" <form>)…)

  A `base` names a head of the branch on the remote that the claim builds on (D167); readers that
  do not know it skip it.

  - `push` uploads one level-0 bundle with the objects the remote lacks and a claim for the
    branch head. Names are content hashes, so writes never conflict and nothing is overwritten.
